#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046669} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559941} :activity/id #uuid "f581d1cd-d5e5-52a7-8233-15cabecbaa60" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050205 :topic/id #uuid "36128875-8025-5e37-8397-54d8247d47c9" :topic/math-academy-id 3131} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559900 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388618 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "220208ba-fab8-55a0-8a2f-2eb8fb671d38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{v}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388620} {:answer/id #uuid "5dde93b7-ebe5-5d5f-8618-0c649d764704" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388621} {:answer/id #uuid "d8598738-9382-5b1a-b55a-73d18649f6d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "A{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388622} {:answer/id #uuid "f6853d60-cd68-56ae-8530-5d5610a55167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "A{v}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388623} {:answer/id #uuid "fce765ef-ed14-5c1c-a75f-4c9e2f5446a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "A{A}^{T}{v}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388624}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388621} :answer-field/id #uuid "e12f6b66-e0b5-5c3d-87c1-2792f81acfc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "82b609f1-ebbe-5986-a6ff-a556a13681d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-139549" :question/problem "![](images/7a/7a2aea092b82dd217b848abdfc162425f098bc13c53975ccfced3f60a4e2ebfb.png)\n\nA $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ which of the following is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $16$ of ${A}^{T}A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=8={\\sigma }_{1},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{1}={8}^{2}=64,$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=4={\\sigma }_{2},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{2}={4}^{2}=16.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=16$ of ${A}^{T}A$ is ${v}_{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "0a2276bc-3326-51a6-9c69-6590d1bce6ac" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559932}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559919 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369741 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07e2bd31-1a71-5fa9-80bb-5391989330e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369743} {:answer/id #uuid "f61dae6c-bf45-5d4f-8713-28f56aef6853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369744} {:answer/id #uuid "8563b272-2537-5a12-ba5d-b5eebc0e5b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369745} {:answer/id #uuid "9e80d123-7a67-5ff4-beb6-4b523e939500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369746} {:answer/id #uuid "d9f5cee2-8204-52ac-a8d9-4f4e81a7c0ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369747}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369745} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b618b69-53f8-5721-89d5-02c9360f4137" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dd29d329-0104-5ef8-b69c-cd8c21549714" :question/math-academy-id "q-139544" :question/problem "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=8,$ ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{5},$ and ${\\sigma }_{3}=0,$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=8,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={8}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}={8}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=8.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=8.$"} :step/id #uuid "417441ef-0974-5094-9fdd-ef33aedcf95b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559967}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559932 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369748 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5508c57-b781-54c4-ac3c-ea5f7e99fa1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{v}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369750} {:answer/id #uuid "9ad78cd4-8bf5-5788-bd3c-9fdda0f21f36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369751} {:answer/id #uuid "9ec95e10-23b1-53ad-b883-d230280ba441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{v}_{1}+2{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369752} {:answer/id #uuid "6bc9be44-ee1f-5d95-aa5a-ac6bdff702eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369753} {:answer/id #uuid "e55ed1bd-18b5-554f-9082-1e45710378cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369754}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369750} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b2343b2-0201-5c51-9b20-71321c6397b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "17f11a2f-af39-5dc3-9819-9bfbdefde21e" :question/math-academy-id "q-139553" :question/problem "![](images/f9/f9b7a55ee1b8d3327ae9da740feaafde459daaee4e42a27cf26fd0ac8e63f20b.png)\n\nA $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ which of the following is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $36$ of ${A}^{T}A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=6={\\sigma }_{1},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{1}={6}^{2}=36,$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=2={\\sigma }_{2},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{2}={2}^{2}=4.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=36$ of ${A}^{T}A$ is ${v}_{1}.$"} :step/id #uuid "57834937-e3aa-51d7-a2de-72481b9133d9"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559941 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "371aa83b-7675-5a37-b450-ceb326f383dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266620} {:answer/id #uuid "3b20f717-34f8-50ab-b29d-bd468f139b4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266666} {:answer/id #uuid "8eb66304-70cb-53f0-b508-f2f4cfc15286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267415} {:answer/id #uuid "9f4e98f1-3aab-5869-8b67-d9a7f948bf2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267582} {:answer/id #uuid "e3a2dd40-7b9e-5445-928c-b6bb8a1dc03d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268153}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266666} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebf581fc-936a-577e-8513-af09419c38fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0157c09c-4c34-5a09-a678-edce758d03a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-139543" :question/problem "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=12,$ ${\\sigma }_{2}=9,$ and ${\\sigma }_{3}=0,$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=12,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={12}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}={12}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=12.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=12.$"} :step/id #uuid "6f93c1d3-714a-571d-89f2-1248ede639db" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559919}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559967 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400763 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72d8c257-9208-59a3-b8ef-e84226b5d7cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$5$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400765} {:answer/id #uuid "c56b5625-8f3f-5546-bd45-82c8dcf4f2c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$25$ and $9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400766} {:answer/id #uuid "9f361d5f-db40-531a-8f20-5389f05b015d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{7}$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400767} {:answer/id #uuid "c82a5223-3bd0-5bad-9e6b-edd5899707f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$11$ and $7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400768} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a6a4f9-ba6e-5a5b-9c4a-a88207909379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$6$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400769}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400765} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dac3c89-e776-5135-b836-fafd4c581933" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "925e17f0-1f91-564c-b150-3d5633a84c6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-139546" :question/problem "![](images/6d/6df3639cb222756221803446ebc735cf1e7cbcf4a922ed7fce835c0c44fb9aa1.png)\n\nA $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ what are the nonzero singular values of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=5={\\sigma }_{1},$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=3={\\sigma }_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.$"} :step/id #uuid "c3193284-a25f-5adc-93c6-8cfd1305db8b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559900}}] :activity/title "The Singular Values of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559013}]}
